## Why This Is Asked
Tests a two-dimensional DP with an inner precomputation and path reconstruction, a common interview pattern for text/partition problems.

## Key Concepts
- Palindromic edit-cost precomputation (costPal).
- Partition DP with exactly k segments.
- Reconstruction of cut points and time/space trade-offs.

## Code Example
```javascript
function minPalPartitionCost(s, k) {
  const n = s.length;
  const costPal = Array.from({ length: n }, () => Array(n).fill(0));
  for (let i = n - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
    for (let j = i; j < n; j++) {
      if (i >= j) costPal[i][j] = 0;
      else costPal[i][j] = costPal[i + 1][j - 1] + (s[i] !== s[j] ? 1 : 0);
    }
  }
  const INF = 1e9;
  const dp = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(INF));
  const par = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(-1));
  dp[0][0] = 0;
  for (let x = 1; x <= n; x++) {
    for (let t = 1; t <= k; t++) {
      for (let i = t - 1; i < x; i++) {
        const val = dp[i][t - 1] + costPal[i][x - 1];
        if (val < dp[x][t]) {
          dp[x][t] = val;
          par[x][t] = i;
        }
      }
    }
  }
  // reconstruction can follow from par
  return { cost: dp[n][k], cuts: par };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How to adapt for at most k substrings instead of exactly k?
- What optimizations reduce time to O(n^2) or enable pruning of the i-loop?

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests proficiency with partition dynamic programming where subarrays must be non-overlapping and values derive from range statistics. The solution requires careful separation of concerns: precomputing range queries, implementing the DP transition, and reconstructing the solution through pointer tracking.

## Key Concepts
- Non-overlapping contiguous subarrays as partition elements
- Dynamic programming with two-dimensional state tracking
- Range minima preprocessing for O(1) query access
- Solution reconstruction through backpointers
- Complexity optimization through careful algorithm design

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxKSubarraysMin(A, k) {
  const n = A.length;

  // Precompute range minima
  const mins = Array.from({length: n}, () => Array(n).fill(0));
  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    let m = Infinity;
    for (let j = i; j < n; j++) {
      m = Math.min(m, A[j]);
      mins[i][j] = m;
    }
  }
```

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to derive a multi-interval DP with per-interval cost and reconstruction. Requires careful state definition and an O(nk) optimization akin to advanced interval DP.

## Key Concepts

- DP over prefixes and interval counts
- Prefix sums for subarray sums
- Running maximum optimization to avoid O(n^2k)
- Path reconstruction to recover subarray boundaries

## Code Example

```javascript
function maxKSubarrays(A, k, c) {
  const n = A.length;
  const S = Array(n+1).fill(0);
  for (let i=1; i<=n; i++) S[i] = S[i-1] + A[i-1];
  const NEG = -1e15;
  const dp = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => Array(k+1).fill(NEG));
  const prv = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => Array(k+1).fill(-1));
  dp[0][0] = 0;
  for (let t=1; t<=k; t++) {
    let best = NEG;
    for (let i=1; i<=n; i++) {
      const val = dp[i-1][t-1] - S[i-1] + c*(i-1);
      if (val > best) { best = val; }
      const cand = S[i] - c*i + best;
      if (cand > dp[i][t]) { dp[i][t] = cand; prv[i][t] = i; }
      if (dp[i-1][t] > dp[i][t]) { dp[i][t] = dp[i-1][t]; prv[i][t] = -1; }
    }
  }
  // reconstruct (simple backtrack)
  const intervals = [];
  let i = n, t = k;
  while (t > 0 && i > 0) {
    if (prv[i][t] === -1) { i--; continue; }
    const end = i;
    const start = prv[i][t];
    intervals.push([start, end]);
    i = start - 1; t--;
  }
  intervals.reverse();
  return { value: dp[n][k], intervals };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you extend to allow variable per-interval cost or rewards based on length?
- How would you optimize memory to O(nk) and still reconstruct intervals?

## Why This Is Asked
This problem tests the ability to design a dynamic programming solution for array partitioning with a per-segment objective function. It combines partition DP with range maximum queries and reconstruction.

## Key Concepts
- Dynamic programming over array partitions
- Range maximum queries (precomputation table M[a][b])
- Path reconstruction using parent pointers
- Time/space complexity: O(n²k) time, O(nk) space

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxPartition(A, k, c){
  const n = A.length-1; // 1-indexed array
  const M = Array.from({length:n+2}, ()=>Array(n+2).fill(0));

  // Precompute range maximums
  for(let i=1; i<=n; i++){
    M[i][i] = A[i];
    for(let j=i+1; j<=n; j++){
      M[i][j] = Math.max(M[i][j-1], A[j]);
    }
  }

  const dp = Array.from({length:n+1}, ()=>Array(k+1).fill(-Infinity));
  const par = Array.from({length:n+1}, ()=>Array(k+1).fill(-1));
  dp[0][0] = 0;

  for(let t=1; t<=k; t++){
    for(let i=t; i<=n; i++){
      for(let j=t-1; j<i; j++){
        const val = dp[j][t-1] + M[j+1][i] - c;
        if(val > dp[i][t]){
          dp[i][t] = val;
          par[i][t] = j;
        }
      }
    }
  }

  // Reconstruct segments
  const segments = [];
  let curr = n;
  for(let t=k; t>=1; t--){
    const prev = par[curr][t];
    segments.unshift([prev+1, curr]);
    curr = prev;
  }

  return {value: dp[n][k], segments};
}
```

## Optimization Opportunities
- Use sparse table for O(1) range max queries with O(n log n) preprocessing
- Apply divide and conquer optimization if applicable
- Consider rolling array optimization to reduce space to O(n)

## Why This Is Asked
Tests decoupling of interval boundaries from inner optimization and handling exact partitioning with mandatory gaps, mirroring scheduling patterns with local windows.

## Key Concepts
- Precompute best[l][r] = max subarray sum in A[l..r] using Kadane per start
- DP over i, t with a gap constraint: previous end <= l-2
- Reconstruct blocks by storing choices (l, i) for each t
- Space/time trade-offs; discuss rolling arrays and memory reuse

## Code Example
```javascript
// precompute best[l][r]
for (let l = 0; l < n; l++) {
  let cur = 0;
  for (let r = l; r < n; r++) {
    cur = Math.max(cur + A[r], A[r]);
    best[l] = best[l] || [];
    best[l][r] = Math.max(best[l][r-1] ?? -Infinity, cur);
  }
}

// DP (conceptual)
for (let i = 0; i < n; i++)
  for (let t = 1; t <= k; t++) {
    dp[i][t] = max over l<=i of dp[l-2][t-1] + best[l][i];
  }
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How to reduce memory to O(nk) with explicit backpointers?
- How would you adapt if blocks may touch (gap size >= 0) or if k is at most K rather than exactly K?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests DP with an extra resource dimension (shields) on a classic grid path. Demonstrates handling of constrained resources, 3D DP states, and reconstruction.

## Key Concepts
- DP with an additional dimension for resources (k)
- Transition with/without consuming a shield
- Path reconstruction via parent pointers

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxGridPath(grid, K) {
  const n = grid.length, m = grid[0].length;
  const NEG = -Infinity;
  const dp = Array.from({length: n}, () => Array.from({length: m}, () => Array(K+1).fill(NEG)));
  dp[0][0][0] = grid[0][0];
  for (let i=0; i<n; i++) {
    for (let j=0; j<m; j++) {
      for (let k=0; k<=K; k++) {
        const v = grid[i][j];
        if (i>0) dp[i][j][k] = Math.max(dp[i][j][k], dp[i-1][j][k] + v);
        if (j>0) dp[i][j][k] = Math.max(dp[i][j][k], dp[i][j-1][k] + v);
        if (k>0 && v < 0) {
          if (i>0) dp[i][j][k] = Math.max(dp[i][j][k], dp[i-1][j][k-1]);
          if (j>0) dp[i][j][k] = Math.max(dp[i][j][k], dp[i][j-1][k-1]);
        }
      }
    }
  }
  return Math.max(...dp[n-1][m-1]);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you recover one optimal path and its moves?
- How to optimize space to O(n*m*K)?

## Why This Is Asked

This question tests deriving a solvable DP for contiguous partitions with a length-based cost, plus a linear-time optimization and path reconstruction.

## Key Concepts

- Contiguous partition DP
- Prefix sums and O(nk) optimization
- Backtracking to report segment endpoints

## Code Example

```javascript
function maxTotalWithCost(A, k, c) {
  const n = A.length;
  const S = new Array(n+1).fill(0);
  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) S[i] = S[i-1] + A[i-1];
  const dp = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => Array(k+1).fill(-Infinity));
  const par = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => Array(k+1).fill(-1));
  dp[0][0] = 0;
  for (let t = 1; t <= k; t++) {
    let bestVal = -Infinity, bestIdx = -1;
    for (let i = t; i <= n; i++) {
      const j = i - 1;
      if (dp[j][t-1] > -Infinity) {
        const cand = dp[j][t-1] - S[j] + c * j;
        if (cand > bestVal) { bestVal = cand; bestIdx = j; }
      }
      if (bestVal > -Infinity) {
        dp[i][t] = S[i] - c * i + c + bestVal;
        par[i][t] = bestIdx;
      }
    }
  }
  const segments = [];
  let i = n, t = k;
  if (dp[n][k] <= -Infinity/2) return {best: -Infinity, segments: []};
  while (t > 0) {
    const j = par[i][t];
    segments.push([j+1, i]);
    i = j; t--;
  }
  segments.reverse();
  return {best: dp[n][k], segments};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you adapt if c is paid only for non-first element or per segment boundary?
- How would you extend to maximize total value for at most k segments instead of exactly k?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests a practical DP with fixed-length window constraints and reconstruction, mirroring analytics batching scenarios.

## Key Concepts
- DP over prefixes with exact segment count
- Prefix sums for fast interval sums
- Reconstruction path tracking

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxKSubarraysWithBounds(A, k, L, R){
  const n = A.length;
  const P = new Array(n+1).fill(0);
  for(let i=1;i<=n;i++) P[i] = P[i-1] + A[i-1];
  const neg = -1/0; const dp = Array.from({length:n+1}, ()=> Array(k+1).fill(neg));
  for(let i=0;i<=n;i++) dp[i][0] = 0;
  const take = Array.from({length:n+1}, ()=> Array(k+1).fill(-1));
  for(let i=1;i<=n;i++){
    for(let t=1;t<=k;t++){
      dp[i][t] = dp[i-1][t];
      for(let len=L; len<=R && len<=i; len++){
        if (dp[i-len][t-1] !== neg) {
          const val = dp[i-len][t-1] + (P[i]-P[i-len]);
          if (val > dp[i][t]){ dp[i][t] = val; take[i][t] = len; }
        }
      }
    }
  }
  return {maxSum: dp[n][k], take}
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

This question tests dynamic programming skills for selecting exactly k disjoint subarrays with a per-subarray penalty—a common variant in coding interviews that extends standard maximum subarray problems.

## Key Concepts

- **Non-overlapping constraint**: Subarrays must not intersect, handled by the state machine.
- **Dual-state DP**: Maintaining both global (dp) and local (end) states to track ongoing vs. completed selections.
- **Reconstruction**: Tracing back decisions to recover actual subarray endpoints.
- **Optimization**: Balancing time complexity (O(nk)) against space (full vs. rolling arrays).

## Code Example

```javascript
const solve = (A, k, c) => {
  const n = A.length;
  const dp = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(-Infinity));
  const end = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(-Infinity));
  const choice = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(null));

  dp[0][0] = 0;

  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    for (let t = 0; t <= k; t++) {
      // Not taking a subarray ending at i
      if (dp[i-1][t] >= end[i][t]) {
        dp[i][t] = dp[i-1][t];
        choice[i][t] = 'skip';
      } else {
        dp[i][t] = end[i][t];
        choice[i][t] = 'end';
      }

      // Taking/starting subarray at i
      if (t > 0) {
        const startNew = dp[i-1][t-1] + A[i-1] - c;
        const extend = end[i-1][t] + A[i-1] - c;
        end[i][t] = Math.max(extend, startNew);
      }
    }
  }

  // Reconstruction logic would go here
  return { value: dp[n][k], subarrays: [] };
};
```

## Complexity

- **Time:** O(nk)
- **Space:** O(nk) for full DP, O(k) for optimized version

## Why This Is Asked
This problem extends the classic Longest Palindromic Subsequence (LPS) problem by allowing up to k character deletions, requiring a 3D DP formulation. It tests understanding of subsequence DP with additional constraints, careful boundary handling, and the ability to reconstruct the solution path.

## Key Concepts
- Longest Palindromic Subsequence with deletions
- 3D DP (i, j, d) where d represents remaining deletions
- Path reconstruction for deletion decisions
- Time/space complexity: O(n²·k) time, O(n²·k) space

## Code Example
```javascript
function longestPalindromicSubseqWithDeletions(s, k) {
  const n = s.length;
  const dp = Array.from({ length: n }, () =>
    Array.from({ length: n }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(0))
  );

  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    for (let d = 0; d <= k; d++) {
      dp[i][i][d] = 1;
    }
  }

  for (let len = 2; len <= n; len++) {
    for (let i = 0; i + len <= n; i++) {
      const j = i + len - 1;
      for (let d = 0; d <= k; d++) {
        if (s[i] === s[j]) {
          dp[i][j][d] = Math.max(dp[i][j][d], (len > 2 ? dp[i + 1][j - 1][d] : 0) + 2);
        }
        if (d > 0) {
          dp[i][j][d] = Math.max(dp[i][j][d], dp[i + 1][j][d - 1], dp[i][j - 1][d - 1]);
        }
      }
    }
  }

  return dp[0][n - 1][k];
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests DP with a range-based segment cost and path reconstruction, a common advanced pattern not covered yet.

## Key Concepts
- Contiguous partitioning
- Range query (max-min)
- DP with backtracking
- Time/space trade-offs and potential optimizations

## Code Example
```javascript
function minCostPartition(A, k) {
  const n = A.length;
  const dp = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(Infinity));
  const cut = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(-1));
  // base
  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    let mx = -Infinity, mn = Infinity;
    for (let p = i - 1; p >= 0; p--) {
      mx = Math.max(mx, A[p]);
      mn = Math.min(mn, A[p]);
      dp[i][1] = Math.max(...A.slice(0, i)) - Math.min(...A.slice(0, i));
    }
  }
  // transitions would fill dp[i][t] and cut[i][t]
  return { cost: dp[n][k], partition: cut };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you optimize range(p, i-1) queries to O(1) per transition?
- How would you adapt for 1-indexed arrays or allow at most k segments?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests DP on ordered subsequences with reconstruction, a common interview pattern that scales gracefully from beginner to moderate.

## Key Concepts
- State dp[i][t] represents best sum ending at i as the t-th pick
- Transition uses max over previous indices; reconstruction via parent pointers
- Space/time trade-offs; potential O(nk) optimization via running maxima

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxDotSubsequence(A,B,k){
  const n=A.length;
  const dp= Array.from({length:n}, ()=> Array(k+1).fill(-Infinity));
  const par= Array.from({length:n}, ()=> Array(k+1).fill(-1));
  for(let i=0;i<n;i++){ dp[i][1] = A[i]*B[i]; par[i][1] = -1; }
  for(let t=2;t<=k;t++){
    for(let i=0;i<n;i++){
      let best=-Infinity; let arg=-1;
      for(let j=0;j<i;j++){
        if(dp[j][t-1] > best){ best=dp[j][t-1]; arg=j; }
      }
      if(best>-Infinity){
        dp[i][t] = best + A[i]*B[i];
        par[i][t] = arg;
      }
    }
  }
  let bestEnd=-1, bestVal=-Infinity;
  for(let i=0;i<n;i++) if(dp[i][k] > bestVal){ bestVal=dp[i][k]; bestEnd=i; }
  const indices=[]; let i=bestEnd, t=k;
  while(i>=0 && t>=1){ indices.push(i); i=par[i][t]; t--; }
  indices.reverse();
  return {value: bestVal, indices};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you optimize to O(nk) time and O(nk) space using prefix maxima per t-1?
- How would you extend to handle k up to n with space-saving tricks?

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests a DP over partitioned subarrays with a non-standard per-subarray score and reconstruction.

## Key Concepts
- DP on non-overlapping subarrays
- Prefix sums and range minimums
- Path reconstruction and base cases
- Complexity: O(n^2 k) time, O(nk) space

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxScore(A, k){
  const n = A.length; const P=[0]; for(let i=0;i<n;i++) P[i+1]=P[i]+A[i];
  const dp=Array.from({length:n+1},()=>Array(k+1).fill(-Infinity));
  const prev=Array.from({length:n+1},()=>Array(k+1).fill(-1));
  for(let i=1;i<=n;i++) dp[i][1]=P[i]-Math.min(...A.slice(0,i));
  for(let t=2;t<=k;t++){
    for(let i=t;i<=n;i++){
      for(let p=t-1;p<=i-1;p++){
        const minVal=Math.min(...A.slice(p,i));
        const val=dp[p][t-1]+(P[i]-P[p])-minVal;
        if(val>dp[i][t]){ dp[i][t]=val; prev[i][t]=p; }
      }
    }
  }
  // reconstruction would trace back using prev
  return dp[n][k];
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How to optimize min queries? N log N via RMQ, or a deque per i.
- How to reconstruct the exact subarrays efficiently?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to model constraints with DP, including exact count, non-adjacency, and reconstruction; this is a common interview pattern for medium-difficulty tasks.

## Key Concepts

- 2D DP with adjacency constraint
- Exact-count optimization
- Backtracking to recover indices

## Code Example

```javascript
function maxPromo(revenue, k, cost){
  const n = revenue.length;
  const NEG = Number.NEGATIVE_INFINITY;
  const dp = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => Array(k+1).fill(NEG));
  dp[0][0] = 0;
  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++){
    for (let t = 0; t <= k; t++){
      // skip i
      dp[i][t] = Math.max(dp[i][t], dp[i-1][t]);
      // take i
      if (t > 0){
        const takeFrom = i >= 2 ? dp[i-2][t-1] : (t-1 === 0 ? 0 : NEG);
        const val = takeFrom + revenue[i-1] - cost;
        dp[i][t] = Math.max(dp[i][t], val);
      }
    }
  }
  // reconstruction
  let i = n, t = k;
  const picks = [];
  while (i > 0 && t >= 0){
    if (dp[i][t] === dp[i-1][t]){ i--; }
    else { picks.unshift(i); i -= 2; t--; }
  }
  return { best: dp[n][k], picks };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you optimize space to O(k)?
- How to handle ties in reconstruction?

## Why This Is Asked
This problem tests a foundational dynamic programming pattern—selecting k non-adjacent elements with maximum sum. It assesses understanding of state definition, transition logic, and reconstruction, making it a classic beginner DP problem.

## Key Concepts
- **State definition**: dp[i][j] represents the optimal sum for the first i elements with exactly j selections
- **Transition logic**: Compare skipping the current element vs. taking it (with the appropriate previous state)
- **Backtracking**: Reconstruct chosen indices by comparing states during backtracking
- **Feasibility constraint**: Maximum selections possible in i elements is floor((i+1)/2)

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxSumNonAdjacent(A, m) {
  const n = A.length;
  const NEG = -Infinity;
  const dp = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(m + 1).fill(NEG));

  for (let i = 0; i <= n; i++) dp[i][0] = 0;

  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    for (let j = 1; j <= Math.min(m, Math.floor((i + 1) / 2)); j++) {
      const take = A[i - 1] + (i >= 2 ? dp[i - 2][j - 1] : (j - 1 === 0 ? 0 : NEG));
      const skip = dp[i - 1][j];
      dp[i][j] = Math.max(skip, take);
    }
  }

  const result = dp[n][m];
  const indices = [];
  let i = n, j = m;

  while (i > 0 && j > 0) {
    const take = A[i - 1] + (i >= 2 ? dp[i - 2][j - 1] : (j - 1 === 0 ? 0 : NEG));
    if (dp[i][j] === take) {
      indices.push(i - 1);
      j--;
      i -= 2;
    } else {
      i--;
    }
  }

  return { sum: result, indices: indices.reverse() };
}
```

**Time Complexity:** O(n × m)
**Space Complexity:** O(n × m), or O(m) with optimization.

## Why This Is Asked
Tests constrained path DP with an extra state (turns). Encourages explicit DP state design, directional transitions, and plan for path reconstruction.

## Key Concepts
- Grid DP with 4D state (i, j, t, dir)
- Transition logic depends on last direction
- Reconstruction via parent pointers
- Edge cases when k is large vs. maximum turns (n+m-2)

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxPathWithTurns(grid, k){
  const n = grid.length, m = grid[0].length;
  const NEG = -1e15;
  const dp = Array.from({length:n}, ()=>
    Array.from({length:m}, ()=>
      Array.from({length:k+1}, ()=> Array(2).fill(NEG))))
  dp[0][0][0][0] = grid[0][0];
  dp[0][0][0][1] = grid[0][0];
  for(let i=0;i<n;i++){
    for(let j=0;j<m;j++){
      for(let t=0;t<=k;t++){
        for(let dir=0;dir<2;dir++){
          const cur = dp[i][j][t][dir];
          if(cur===NEG) continue;
          // move right
          if(j+1<m){
            const nt = t + (dir===0?0:1);
            if(nt<=k) dp[i][j+1][nt][0] = Math.max(dp[i][j+1][nt][0], cur + grid[i][j+1]);
          }
          // move down
          if(i+1<n){
            const nt = t + (dir===1?0:1);
            if(nt<=k) dp[i+1][j][nt][1] = Math.max(dp[i+1][j][nt][1], cur + grid[i+1][j]);
          }
        }
      }
    }
  }
  let ans = NEG;
  for(let t=0;t<=k;t++){
    ans = Math.max(ans, dp[n-1][m-1][t][0], dp[n-1][m-1][t][1]);
  }
  return ans;
}
```

